ATTRIBUTION AND SCOPE OF REUSE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Apache-2.0 §4 requires that notices be retained in copied files. Two layers, both
required, per the ruling in Core -> Canary Response 001 §4:

  1. This root NOTICE names the source repository, commit, author and license.
  2. Every transplanted file additionally carries a per-file provenance header
     giving its upstream path, the commit it came from, and a "modified" flag
     where it has been changed.

The finer grain is deliberate. This programme's thesis is that provenance is
recorded at the finest grain that stays true; its own source tree does not get a
coarser standard than the receipts it emits.

No upstream source file has been copied. The assessment in
docs/to_core/Assessment_001_2026-08-21.md §8 recommended building the five run-loop
components new and harvesting disciplines, patterns and offline fixtures rather
than code, and that is what C2 did.

Two things in this tree are nevertheless DERIVED from upstream and carry per-file
provenance headers, because attribution is owed to derived work and not only to
copied bytes:

  canary/suite/refusal.py   The three refusal-sentinel classification RULES (v1, v2,
                            v3) are upstream's, preserved exactly, including each
                            boundary they refuse to cross. The code is new: upstream's
                            are module-level predicates carrying a cross-repository
                            import of v1's identity, and this engine needs a
                            self-contained instrument object whose version can enter
                            an i_digest. Marked REIMPLEMENTED, NOT COPIED -- and
                            validated by reproducing upstream's published counts to
                            the unit over upstream's own frozen replies.

  fixtures/paper2/*.json    Byte-for-byte copies of upstream telemetry artifacts,
                            pinned by fixtures/paper2.SHA256 and documented in
                            fixtures/paper2/PROVENANCE.md. These are RECEIVED data:
                            copied verbatim, never edited, never re-serialised.

If a file is ever copied rather than derived, its header lands with it in the same
commit, never afterwards.

